{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && ls -1 | rg 'blind|adjudi|r66|r65' ; ls -R poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23\"","aggregated_output":"blind4.py\nblind5.py\nblind6.py\nblind_adjudicate.py\nblind_reread.py\nblind_reread2.py\nblind_rules.py\nblind_test.py\nclaude-r65.md\ncodex-r65.md\ncodex-r65.stream\ncodex-r66.md\ncodex-r66.stream\nls: poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23: No such file or directory\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'ls -R poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reasons.json; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X08.codex.json; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X08.claude.json'","aggregated_output":"ls: poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23: No such file or directory\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"X01\": {\n     3\t  \"first\": \"compatible\",\n     4\t  \"note\": \"Both: H1 reported, Q3 from guidance and the beat record, 92.5% share held; even flat Q4 keeps the year at or above $350B, and $400B needs about 27.6% Q4 sequential growth against the guided pace and supply limits.\"\n     5\t },\n     6\t \"X02\": {\n     7\t  \"first\": \"compatible\",\n     8\t  \"note\": \"Both apply D747: e1947 covers the same four companies and year; the answer explains why most of that spending buys data-center equipment and facilities (e1649 memory, e4405 Azure backlog) and why falling below $700B would need more than $30B to go elsewhere or slip.\"\n     9\t },\n    10\t \"X03\": {\n    11\t  \"first\": \"compatible\",\n    12\t  \"note\": \"Both: the 10% shortfall has no size or grounding in shipment timing; the cited passages name customers, an evaluation and a benchmark, not lost or slipped revenue.\"\n    13\t },\n    14\t \"X04\": {\n    15\t  \"first\": \"compatible\",\n    16\t  \"note\": \"Both: $350B needs only $186B of second-half segment revenue, below the Q2 run-rate plus the guided step-up; $400B needs $236B, more than the forecast total company second half.\"\n    17\t },\n    18\t \"X06\": {\n    19\t  \"first\": \"compatible\",\n    20\t  \"note\": \"Both: the guided figure sits on the $40B line on a projected base; no passage gives shipments, orders with units and prices, or a deployment schedule, so neither a shortfall nor an overshoot can be sized and the rung stays unsettled.\"\n    21\t },\n    22\t \"X07\": {\n    23\t  \"first\": \"compatible\",\n    24\t  \"note\": \"Both no on support (not scored for this control): the 2% residual and the Microsoft uplift that decide the band are unsized, and the answer's own 96% bound gives rung 3. The floor defect is marked by one reader only and goes to adjudication.\"\n    25\t },\n    26\t \"X08\": {\n    27\t  \"first\": \"incompatible\",\n    28\t  \"note\": \"Corrected after council 65 (my error: I noted the tension and still recorded compatible). The Claude reader says data-center capex is at least the B AI-infrastructure figure and accepts a .4B outcome; the Codex reader treats B as a guide that permits a lower outcome. The two readings disagree on whether the published number is possible, so the registered procedure sends X08 to adjudication.\",\n    29\t  \"adjudicated\": \"incompatible\",\n    30\t  \"adjudicated_note\": \"Both adjudicators say yes. The Claude adjudicator again reads e1947 as a floor (\\\"data-center spending is at least that figure\\\") while accepting .4B; the Codex adjudicator counts the infrastructure as data-center investment and applies the 96% share without reconciling the B figure. Under council 65, a reading that cites a floor above the outcome is not compatible with one that treats the figure as a guide, so the support stays unsettled.\"\n    31\t }\n    32\t}     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"At the forecast total of $740 billion, spending outside data centers would have to exceed $40 billion to put the outcome below rung 4. The same four companies indicated they would spend “more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year” (e1947). The writer counts that infrastructure as data-center investment. Meta’s $130 billion AI plan against the $135 billion midpoint of its total guidance (e3686, e1549) gives a concrete comparison for the writer’s estimated 96% share. Applying that share leaves $29.6 billion outside data centers and puts data-center spending at $710.4 billion, within rung 4.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": []\n     9\t}     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The outcome is F18 = share(F16 $740bn, F17 96%) = $710.4bn. The arithmetic is correct, and the result sits in rung 4 ($700-800bn). The total is placed inside the quoted range 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion' (e5014, e2079), just below the trackers 'near $745 billion' (e1856) and '$760 billion' (e2458). The share is what decides the band: rung 4 holds as long as data centers take at least about 94.6% of $740bn. The share rests on a related figure that the answer explains. e1947 says the same four companies 'have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year', which matches the companies and the period. The answer gives its reason for the quantity: data-center capex 'contains all AI capex plus non-AI cloud capacity', so data-center spending is at least that figure, above the $700bn line. It adds Meta's '$130 billion of AI capex' (e3686) against the $135bn midpoint of '$125 billion to $145 billion' (e1549), about 96%. It notes that Amazon's raise was 'mostly due to higher memory chip costs' (e1649). It also cites the 'around $750 billion' of data-center spending in e4082 and the '$750 billion' in e1416. The answer says rung 3 would need a non-data-center remainder above about 5% (about $40bn) and admits the margin is thin. Under its own stated assumptions it explains why rung 4 is the more likely side. That meets the requirement for an explained related figure, even though rung 3 is still possible.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"tail -3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md; rg -n -m 4 'D750' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md docs/DECISIONS.md; cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'def first_json|WRITERS|def build_case' blind_test.py; rg -n 'reasons|floor|volume in hand' claude-r65.md; shasum -a 256 judging-v13s{,-d749}.txt\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"tail -3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md; rg -n -m 4 'D750' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md docs/DECISIONS.md; cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'def first_json|WRITERS|def build_case' blind_test.py; rg -n 'reasons|floor|volume in hand' claude-r65.md; shasum -a 256 judging-v13s{,-d749}.txt\"","aggregated_output":"| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1094:| D750 | 2026-11-23 | D749 writer test INCONCLUSIVE, no failed line: E Codex now withholds with a specific gap (both accept), both P-v8 and S Codex supported, both controls caught; splits on X05 (E Claude takes a not-yet-shipping target in full; no passage shows volume in hand) and X08 (S Claude, floor vs guide reading; my compatibility record corrected after council 65); council 65 under D439 (Alex asleep, \"自行收敛\"): bullet 2 \"it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence puts the required volume in hand for the period\", closed list rejected; second $0 writer test on S, E, P-v8 with the same controls and lines; paid re-send on hold | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND5-RESULT.md` |\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:448:**The one next task: the second $0 writer test (D750), after council round 66 reviews it.** Alex is asleep and handed decisions to\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:459:- **The test.** The same design on S, E and P-v8 (`blind6.py`; `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D750]).\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:464:**Earlier next task (done, D750): the $0 writer test of the E writer repair (D749), after council round 64 reviews the request\n64:def build_case(name, spec):\n105:WRITERS = ('claude', 'codex')\n109:def first_json(text):\n120:    return [f'{name}/{w}' for name in CASES for w in WRITERS if not (out / name / 'answers' / f'{w}.json').exists()]\n130:        for w in WRITERS:\n195:    if len(rows) != len(CASES) * len(WRITERS):\n196:        raise SystemExit(f'INCOMPLETE: {len(rows)} checked answers, {len(CASES) * len(WRITERS)} registered')\n20:   when the evidence shows the volume in hand\".\n23:   - It is not a gap in D747. The two families differ on whether an outlook counts as volume in hand.\n25:   - The earlier E failures were invented cuts. This one is the opposite: no discount, by asserting volume in hand.\n26:   - The request's clause \"the evidence shows the volume in hand\" has no closed definition, and AGENTS §6 says \"a classification\n28:   - The smallest structural fix is a closed definition at its owner (bullet 2): volume in hand means reported shipments or revenue\n44:- **Option 3.** Put \"is a reaffirmed outlook volume in hand?\" to Alex as a ruling. This is a definitional question his existing words\n45:  already answer (\"taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand\"). I lean that the council can settle it reversibly\n51:  that E's passages do show volume in hand.\n54f5847d715caec159e6509b7966c13df4d4af0756953d206fefa1347879f397  judging-v13s.txt\nac707fa96890cee1f69dcf84e6c5242ec014b00b502214ece84b1318252a9859  judging-v13s-d749.txt\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba blind_adjudicate.py | head -210 && nl -ba blind_test.py | sed -n '15,121p'; rg -n 'adjudicat|floor' blind_reread2.py | head -40\ncd ../../.. && for f in E S; do rg -n 'e3660|e1947' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/\"'$f/user.txt | cut -c 1-500; done\nrg -n '\"'already selling|contains all AI|96%|volume in hand' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/{X05,X08}.md | tail -16 | cut -c 1-650\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba blind_adjudicate.py | head -210 && nl -ba blind_test.py | sed -n '15,121p'; rg -n 'adjudicat|floor' blind_reread2.py | head -40\ncd ../../.. && for f in E S; do rg -n 'e3660|e1947' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/\"'$f/user.txt | cut -c 1-500; done\nrg -n '\"'already selling|contains all AI|96%|volume in hand' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/{X05,X08}.md | tail -16 | cut -c 1-650\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"Adjudication and refusal-review packets for a judging-v13s blind test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5, D742).\n     2\t\n     3\tAdjudication: for every reply whose two readers disagree (a member's \"supported\", or any defect one reader marks), one packet holding\n     4\tthe two readings, the system text, the format rules the writer was given (every schema description), the request as the writer saw it\n     5\tand the answer. Reader 1 is the Codex reading and Reader 2 the Claude reading; the adjudicators are not told which.\n     6\t\n     7\tRefusal review: for every refused first reply, one packet with the refusal messages, the system text, the schema, the user text and the\n     8\tanswer, asking the two registered judgements apart: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound, and does the exact request\n     9\tclearly state the condition the refusal enforces (D742: a sound answer refused under a clearly stated condition still stops the run).\n    10\t\n    11\tUsage: python3 blind_adjudicate.py <blind test folder> <packet id> [<packet id> ...]\n    12\t       python3 blind_adjudicate.py refusal <blind test folder> <case> <writer>\n    13\t\"\"\"\n    14\timport json\n    15\timport sys\n    16\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    17\t\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\n    19\tfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, descriptions, format_material  # noqa: E402\n    20\t\n    21\tHEADER = \"\"\"# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\n    22\t\n    23\tTwo readers disagreed about the answer below. Settle each point against the passages in the request and the format rules quoted\n    24\tbelow, not against either reader's threshold. Read only what is given here.\n    25\t\n    26\tFor each member, decide **supported** (yes or no). For each defect a reader marked, decide **uphold** or **reject**, quoting the\n    27\tpassage words or the format rule that decides it.\n    28\t\n    29\t\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n    30\t\n    31\tReturn only JSON: {\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\"}},\n    32\t \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1|Reader 2\", \"type\": \"...\", \"figures\": [...], \"decision\": \"uphold|reject\", \"why\": \"...\"}]}\n    33\t\"\"\"\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\tdef _json(text):\n    37\t    \"\"\"A reading: the outermost JSON object in the file.\"\"\"\n    38\t    return json.loads(text[text.find('{'):text.rfind('}') + 1])\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\tdef reading_lines(label, reading):\n    42\t    lines = []\n    43\t    for member, mark in sorted(reading.get('members', {}).items()):\n    44\t        line = f\"- {label} on member {member}: supported = {mark.get('supported')}. Ground: {mark.get('ground', '')}\"\n    45\t        if mark.get('why_not'):\n    46\t            line += f\" Why not: {mark['why_not']}\"\n    47\t        lines.append(line)\n    48\t    defects = reading.get('defects', [])\n    49\t    if not defects:\n    50\t        lines.append(f'- {label} marks no defect.')\n    51\t    for defect in defects:\n    52\t        lines.append(f\"- {label} marks a defect: {defect.get('type')} (figures {defect.get('figures', [])}): {defect.get('detail', '')}\")\n    53\t    return lines\n    54\t\n    55\t\n    56\tdef build(folder, packet_ids, key_name='reader-key.json', packets='reader-packets', out_name='adjudication'):\n    57\t    key = json.loads((folder / key_name).read_text())\n    58\t    out = folder / out_name\n    59\t    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    60\t    for pid in packet_ids:\n    61\t        case, writer = key[pid]['case'], key[pid]['writer']\n    62\t        codex = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.codex.json').read_text())\n    63\t        claude = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.claude.json').read_text())\n    64\t        answer_path = folder / key[pid]['answer'] if key[pid].get('answer') else folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json'\n    65\t        case_dir = Path(key[pid]['case_dir']) if key[pid].get('case_dir') else folder / case   # D747: the writer's own request\n    66\t        text = '\\n'.join([HEADER, '# The two readings', *reading_lines('Reader 1', codex), *reading_lines('Reader 2', claude), '',\n    67\t                          format_material(case_dir), '',\n    68\t                          '# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)', (case_dir / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n    69\t                          '# The answer', answer_path.read_text()])\n    70\t        target = out / f'{pid}.md'\n    71\t        if target.exists():\n    72\t            raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n    73\t        target.write_text(text)\n    74\t        (out / f'{pid}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n    75\t                                               'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n    76\t        print(f'{pid}: {case}/{writer}, {len(text)} chars')\n    77\t\n    78\t\n    79\tREFUSAL_HEADER = \"\"\"# Refusal review (judging-v13s blind test)\n    80\t\n    81\tA code check refused the answer below. For each refusal message, give two judgements apart, from the request text and the passages\n    82\talone:\n    83\t1. sound: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound against the passages (would an analyst accept the number and the reasons\n    84\t   that produce it)? Judge this on its own, apart from the format rule the check enforces. Answer yes or no, with why.\n    85\t2. stated: does the exact request (system text, schema or user text below) clearly state the condition the refusal enforces? Answer\n    86\t   yes or no, and quote the sentence that states it, or say none does.\n    87\tReturn only JSON: {\"refusals\": [{\"message\": \"...\", \"sound\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\", \"stated\": \"yes|no\", \"sentence\": \"...\"}]}\n    88\t\"\"\"\n    89\t\n    90\t\n    91\tdef refusal(folder, case, writer):\n    92\t    checked = {(r['case'], r['writer']): r for r in json.loads((folder / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())}\n    93\t    row = checked[(case, writer)]\n    94\t    if row.get('decision') == 'publish' or not row.get('errors'):\n    95\t        raise SystemExit(f'{case}/{writer} was not refused')\n    96\t    out = folder / 'refusal-review'\n    97\t    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    98\t    text = '\\n'.join([REFUSAL_HEADER, '# Refusal messages', json.dumps(row['errors'], ensure_ascii=False), '',\n    99\t                      '# The system text', (folder / case / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n   100\t                      '# The schema', (folder / case / 'schema.json').read_text(), '',\n   101\t                      '# The user text (the request, with every passage)', (folder / case / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n   102\t                      '# The answer', (folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json').read_text()])\n   103\t    target = out / f'{case}-{writer}.md'\n   104\t    if target.exists():\n   105\t        raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n   106\t    target.write_text(text)\n   107\t    (out / f'{case}-{writer}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n   108\t                                                    'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n   109\t    print(f'refusal review {case}/{writer}: {len(text)} chars')\n   110\t\n   111\t\n   112\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   113\t    if len(sys.argv) == 5 and sys.argv[1] == 'refusal':\n   114\t        refusal(Path(sys.argv[2]).resolve(), sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4])\n   115\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread':        # D743: the re-read's packets, key and adjudication folder\n   116\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread-key.json', 'reread-packets', 'reread-adjudication')\n   117\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread2':       # D744: the second re-read under the shared rule text\n   118\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread2-key.json', 'reread2-packets', 'reread2-adjudication')\n   119\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread-d747':   # D747: the re-read under Alex's comparison ruling\n   120\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread-key.json', 'reread-packets', 'reread-adjudication')\n   121\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 3 and sys.argv[1] != 'refusal':\n   122\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[2:])\n   123\t    else:\n   124\t        raise SystemExit(__doc__)\n    15\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    16\t\n    17\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    18\tREPO = HERE.parents[2]\n    19\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    20\tsys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / 'src'))\n    21\timport calc_small as cs  # noqa: E402\n    22\timport paid_gate as pg  # noqa: E402\n    23\tfrom hivemind import model  # noqa: E402\n    24\t\n    25\tW = pg.W\n    26\tCASES = {   # registered in SMALL-DESIGN.md §5: archive, calls, member keys\n    27\t    'P': {'draw': 'old-1', 'calls': [('v4', 8810), ('v8', 8816)], 'arm': 'joint'},\n    28\t    'P-v4': {'draw': 'old-1', 'calls': [('v4', 8810), ('v8', 8816)], 'arm': 'separate', 'member': 'v4'},\n    29\t    'P-v8': {'draw': 'old-1', 'calls': [('v4', 8810), ('v8', 8816)], 'arm': 'separate', 'member': 'v8'},\n    30\t    'S': {'draw': 'old-3', 'calls': [('v6', 9043)], 'arm': 'single',\n    31\t          'what': W('four largest AI hyperscalers', 'data-center capex', 'combined', 'CY2026')},\n    32\t    'O': {'draw': 'old-2', 'calls': [('v29', 8507)], 'arm': 'single', 'what': W('NVIDIA', 'equity investment commitments', 'company', 'end of FY2027'),\n    33\t          'note': pg.O_NOTE},\n    34\t    'E': {'draw': 'old-3', 'calls': [('v41', 9071)], 'arm': 'single', 'what': W('NVIDIA', 'revenue', 'Vera CPU', 'FY2028')},\n    35\t}\n    36\tREGISTERED_QUESTIONS = {\n    37\t    ('old-1', 'v4'): \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\",\n    38\t    ('old-1', 'v8'): 'How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?',\n    39\t    ('old-3', 'v6'): 'How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?',\n    40\t    ('old-2', 'v29'): \"How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\",\n    41\t    ('old-3', 'v41'): 'What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?',\n    42\t}\n    43\tREGISTERED_EDGES = {('old-1', 'v4'): [431.2, 392.0, 352.8, 313.6], ('old-1', 'v8'): [400.0, 350.0, 300.0, 250.0],\n    44\t                    ('old-3', 'v6'): [500.0, 600.0, 700.0, 800.0], ('old-2', 'v29'): [25.0, 50.0, 75.0, 100.0],\n    45\t                    ('old-3', 'v41'): [50.0, 40.0, 30.0, 20.0]}\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef heading(draw, call_id):\n    49\t    a = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{pg.RUN / draw / \"prod/archive.sqlite\"}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    50\t    user = json.loads(a.execute('select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?', (call_id,)).fetchone()[0])['messages'][1]['content']\n    51\t    m = re.match(r'Thesis (v\\d+) \\([^)]*\\): ([^\\n]*)', user)\n    52\t    return m.group(1), m.group(2)\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\tdef edges(bands):\n    56\t    out = []\n    57\t    for lo, hi in bands:\n    58\t        for e in (lo, hi):\n    59\t            if e is not None and e not in out:\n    60\t                out.append(e)\n    61\t    return out\n    62\t\n    63\t\n    64\tdef build_case(name, spec):\n    65\t    draw = spec['draw']\n    66\t    for view_id, call_id in spec['calls']:                     # the registered heading, question and ladder, or refuse\n    67\t        tid, question = heading(draw, call_id)\n    68\t        if tid != view_id or question != REGISTERED_QUESTIONS[(draw, view_id)]:\n    69\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: call {call_id} heads {tid} \"{question}\", not the registered {view_id}')\n    70\t        got = edges(pg.bands(draw, view_id))\n    71\t        if got != REGISTERED_EDGES[(draw, view_id)]:                   # [54] in rung order, not sorted\n    72\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: {view_id} ladder edges {got}, not the registered {REGISTERED_EDGES[(draw, view_id)]}')\n    73\t    if spec['arm'] in ('joint', 'separate'):\n    74\t        (tv, tc), (dv, dc) = spec['calls']\n    75\t        family = pg.small_family(pg.pair_family(draw, (tv, tc), (dv, dc)), pg.PAIR_SCOPES)\n    76\t        if spec['arm'] == 'joint':\n    77\t            return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', 'joint', draw, spec['calls'], family, [tv, dv], 'v13s')\n    78\t        return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', f'separate-{spec[\"member\"]}', draw, spec['calls'], pg.only(family, spec['member']), [spec['member']], 'v13s')\n    79\t    view_id, call_id = spec['calls'][0]\n    80\t    family = {'members': {view_id: {'what': spec['what'], 'bands': pg.bands(draw, view_id)}}, 'relations': [], 'shared': [], 'compare_periods': []}\n    81\t    family = pg.small_family(family, notes={view_id: spec['note']} if spec.get('note') else None)\n    82\t    return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', 'single', draw, spec['calls'], family, [view_id], 'v13s')\n    83\t\n    84\t\n    85\tdef build(out):\n    86\t    if out.exists():\n    87\t        raise SystemExit(f'{out} exists')\n    88\t    gate_quotes = {p['text'] for it in json.loads((HERE / 'paid-plan.json').read_text()) for p in it['passages'].values()}\n    89\t    report = []\n    90\t    for name, spec in CASES.items():\n    91\t        it = build_case(name, spec)\n    92\t        folder = out / name\n    93\t        (folder / 'answers').mkdir(parents=True)\n    94\t        (folder / 'system.txt').write_text(it['system'])\n    95\t        (folder / 'user.txt').write_text(it['user'])\n    96\t        (folder / 'schema.json').write_text(json.dumps(it['schema'], indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n    97\t        (folder / 'item.json').write_text(json.dumps(it, ensure_ascii=False))\n    98\t        fresh = sum(1 for p in it['passages'].values() if p['text'] not in gate_quotes)\n    99\t        report.append(f'{name:5} {it[\"arm\"]:13} members {it[\"members\"]} passages {len(it[\"passages\"])} ({fresh} quotes absent from the gate) '\n   100\t                      f'request {len(it[\"system\"]) + len(it[\"user\"]) + len(json.dumps(it[\"schema\"]))} chars')\n   101\t    (out / 'BUILD.txt').write_text('\\n'.join(report) + '\\n')\n   102\t    print('\\n'.join(report))\n   103\t\n   104\t\n   105\tWRITERS = ('claude', 'codex')\n   106\t_FENCE = re.compile(r'^\\s*```(?:json)?\\s*\\n(.*)\\n\\s*```\\s*$', re.S)\n   107\t\n   108\t\n   109\tdef first_json(text):\n   110\t    \"\"\"[54] The writer's first output exactly as returned: one JSON object, optionally inside a single ```json fence; anything else\n   111\t    (a preface, an array, two objects) is not an answer.\"\"\"\n   112\t    m = _FENCE.match(text)\n   113\t    body = m.group(1) if m else text\n   114\t    data = json.loads(body)\n   115\t    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else None\n   116\t\n   117\t\n   118\tdef inventory(out):\n   119\t    \"\"\"[54] Every registered case has one first answer from each writer.\"\"\"\n   120\t    return [f'{name}/{w}' for name in CASES for w in WRITERS if not (out / name / 'answers' / f'{w}.json').exists()]\n   121\t\n2:adjudicators get (blind_rules: system text, every schema description, the definitions), plus five controls built from fresh copies:\n10:- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n21:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n29:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n29:- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n34:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1947, e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.md:54:- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.md:665:   \"basis_text\": \"Share of the four companies' total capital expenditure that goes to data centers (servers, chips, networking, buildings, power). Data-center capex contains all AI capex plus non-AI cloud capacity. Meta's AI capex alone is $130bn (F15) against the $135bn midpoint of its $125-145bn guidance (F2), about 96% before non-AI data centers; Amazon's raise was mostly server memory cost, i.e. data-center spending; the companies' indicated AI-infrastructure spending exceeds $730bn (F11) and an outside estimate puts the tech giants' data-center spending at $750bn (F12), bo\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.md:713:   \"reasons\": \"Reference: the market expects the four companies' combined 2026 capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F6, Zacks; the same aggregated guidance appears in e2079 and, as more than $730bn of AI infrastructure, in e1947). That range sits in rung 4 ($700-800bn). It is total capital expenditure, so the data-center outcome sits at or slightly below it.\\n\\nBuild of the total: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised in July), Meta $125-145bn (raised) and Amazon $220bn (raised, mostly on memory-chip costs); all three report on the calendar year, so together $540-570bn (F5).\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X05.md:54:- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X05.md:255:   \"basis_text\": \"My forecast pace for FY2028 CPU revenue over FY2027. The benchmark is NVIDIA's guidance that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double (F2, repeated by the CEO), which sets a floor of 2.0x. The base is reaffirmed for the current year, so the line is already selling; stated deployment at every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM and Anthropic's evaluation of Vera for agentic work support a small overshoot. AMD's benchmark claims against Vera trim the upside, so the pace stays well short of the 2.5x that $50 billion would need.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X05.md:360:   \"reasons\": \"Reference: NVIDIA's guidance. The CFO said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 to more than double it (e3660); the CEO and management repeat the same claim (e4764, e4856), and e4691 and e4856 state it as Vera CPU revenue. Two times $20 billion is $40 billion (F3), and 'more than double' puts the reference just inside rung 2 ($40 billion to under $50 billion). Which band the evidence favours: rung 2. The FY2027 base is reaffirmed for the current year, so the CPU line is already selling at volume rather than a product with n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && python3 -B - <<'PY'\nimport hashlib, json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport blind6\nb=blind6.blind5\nold=b.BLIND4.parent/'judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23'\nfor name in b.CASES:\n    it=b.b4.bt.build_case(name,b.b4.bt.CASES[name])\n    prev=json.loads((old/name/'item.json').read_text())\n    print(name, len(it['passages']), [k for k in it.keys()|prev.keys() if it.get(k)\"'!=prev.get(k)],hashlib.sha256(it['\"'system'].encode()).hexdigest()==b.REQUEST_SHA256)\nfor f in ('judging-v13s.txt','judging-v13s-d749.txt'):\n    print(f,len(Path(f).read_text()),len(Path(f).read_bytes()))\nPY\nnl -ba ../../prd/10-filing-phase.md | tail -39\nnl -ba ../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X05.claude.json | cut -c1-750 | head -20\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && python3 -B - <<'PY'\nimport hashlib, json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport blind6\nb=blind6.blind5\nold=b.BLIND4.parent/'judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23'\nfor name in b.CASES:\n    it=b.b4.bt.build_case(name,b.b4.bt.CASES[name])\n    prev=json.loads((old/name/'item.json').read_text())\n    print(name, len(it['passages']), [k for k in it.keys()|prev.keys() if it.get(k)\"'!=prev.get(k)],hashlib.sha256(it['\"'system'].encode()).hexdigest()==b.REQUEST_SHA256)\nfor f in ('judging-v13s.txt','judging-v13s-d749.txt'):\n    print(f,len(Path(f).read_text()),len(Path(f).read_bytes()))\nPY\nnl -ba ../../prd/10-filing-phase.md | tail -39\nnl -ba ../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X05.claude.json | cut -c1-750 | head -20\"","aggregated_output":"zsh:1: can't create temp file for here document: operation not permitted\n   163\t  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n   164\t    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n   165\t  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n   166\t  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n   167\t\n   168\t## 7. Process (no drift)\n   169\t\n   170\t- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n   171\t  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n   172\t  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n   173\t- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n   174\t- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n   175\t  sentence, in both directions.\n   176\t- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n   177\t- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n   178\t\n   179\t## 8. Steps and where we are\n   180\t\n   181\t| # | Step | Output | Status |\n   182\t|---|---|---|---|\n   183\t| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n   184\t| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n   185\t| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n   186\t| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n   187\t| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n   188\t| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n   189\t| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n   190\t| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n   191\t| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n   192\t\n   193\t## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n   194\t\n   195\t- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n   196\t- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n   197\t- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n   198\t- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n   199\t- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n   200\t- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n   201\t- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"e3660 (CFO: still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects it to more than double in FY2028), repeated by e4764 and framed as Vera CPU revenue by e4691 and e4856. The guided floor of 2.0x on $20 billion gives at least $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. The stated assumption F4 (a 2.1x pace: the guided floor plus a small overshoot, with AMD's benchmark claims trimming the upside, well short of the 2.5x that $50 billion needs) gives $42 billion. F6 takes Vera as 100% of FY2028 CPU revenue because e4691 and e4856 describe the doubling as Vera CPU revenue and no passage sizes another CPU line.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py | sed -n '145,198p'; cat poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/user.txt | head -42\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py | sed -n '145,198p'; cat poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/user.txt | head -42\"","aggregated_output":"   145\t                defect_state[d] = 'settled' if pair == ['uphold', 'uphold'] else 'rejected' if pair == ['reject', 'reject'] else 'split'\n   146\t        for d, state in defect_state.items():\n   147\t            row = [pid, item['label'], d[0], list(d[1]), state]\n   148\t            (settled_defects if state == 'settled' else split_defects if state == 'split' else []).append(row)\n   149\t            if fresh and state == 'settled':\n   150\t                fails.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]}: settled defect {d[0]} {list(d[1])}')\n   151\t            if fresh and state == 'split':\n   152\t                splits.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]}: defect split {d[0]} {list(d[1])}')\n   153\t        if floor:\n   154\t            floor_state = defect_state.get((FLOOR_DEFECT[0], ('F12',)), 'missed')\n   155\t        for a in answer['answers']:\n   156\t            view = a['view_id']\n   157\t            votes = [_vote(r['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported'), f'{pid} first reading') for r in first]\n   158\t            if votes[0] == votes[1] and record.get('first') == 'compatible':\n   159\t                value = votes[0]\n   160\t            elif votes[0] == votes[1] and 'first' not in record:\n   161\t                need.append(f'{pid} (reasons not yet recorded)')\n   162\t                continue\n   163\t            elif adj is None:\n   164\t                need.append(f'{pid} (support)')\n   165\t                continue\n   166\t            else:\n   167\t                second = [_vote(x['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported'), f'{pid} adjudication') for x in adj]\n   168\t                votes += second\n   169\t                if second[0] != second[1]:\n   170\t                    value = 'split'\n   171\t                elif 'adjudicated' not in record:\n   172\t                    need.append(f'{pid} (adjudicated reasons not yet recorded)')\n   173\t                    continue\n   174\t                else:\n   175\t                    value = second[0] if record['adjudicated'] == 'compatible' else 'split'\n   176\t            if item['label'] == 'K-invented-cut':\n   177\t                rule, ok = 'control expects not supported', value == 'no'\n   178\t            elif floor:\n   179\t                rule, ok = 'support not scored; the floor defect is', True\n   180\t            elif item['case'] == 'P-v8':\n   181\t                rule, ok = 'a supported forecast', a['shape'] == 'forecast' and value == 'yes'\n   182\t            else:\n   183\t                rule, ok = 'a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer', value == 'yes'\n   184\t            if not ok:\n   185\t                (splits if value == 'split' else fails).append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]}: {view} settled={value} [{rule}]')\n   186\t            lines.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]:22} {view} {a[\"shape\"]:10} settled={value:6} votes={votes} [{rule}] {\"ok\" if ok else \"MISS\"}')\n   187\t    if need:\n   188\t        print('not final; needed:', ' | '.join(sorted(set(need))))\n   189\t        return\n   190\t    if floor_state in ('missed', 'rejected'):\n   191\t        fails.append(f'K-impossible-floor: the floor defect was {floor_state}')\n   192\t    elif floor_state == 'split':\n   193\t        splits.append('K-impossible-floor: the floor defect split')\n   194\t    verdict = 'fail' if fails else 'inconclusive' if splits else 'pass'\n   195\t    result = {'verdict': verdict, 'fails': fails, 'splits': splits, 'lines': lines, 'settled_defects': settled_defects,\n   196\t              'split_defects': split_defects, 'floor_control': floor_state}\n   197\t    (out / 'TALLY.json').write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   198\t    print('\\n'.join(lines))\n[Family] members:\nThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n\nRelations: none.\nShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\nOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n\n[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n- e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n- e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain in the overall SPEC 2026 suite.\n    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && python3 -B -c 'import hashlib, json, blind6\nfrom pathlib import Path\nb=blind6.blind5\nold=b.BLIND4.parent/\\\"judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23\\\"\nfor name in b.CASES:\n    it=b.b4.bt.build_case(name,b.b4.bt.CASES[name])\n    prev=json.loads((old/name/\\\"item.json\\\").read_text())\n    print(name,len(it[\\\"passages\\\"]),[k for k in it.keys()|prev.keys() if it.get(k)\"'!=prev.get(k)],hashlib.sha256(it[\"system\"].encode()).hexdigest()==b.REQUEST_SHA256)\nfor f in (\"judging-v13s.txt\",\"judging-v13s-d749.txt\"):\n    print(f,len(Path(f).read_text()),len(Path(f).read_bytes()))'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && python3 -B -c 'import hashlib, json, blind6\nfrom pathlib import Path\nb=blind6.blind5\nold=b.BLIND4.parent/\\\"judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23\\\"\nfor name in b.CASES:\n    it=b.b4.bt.build_case(name,b.b4.bt.CASES[name])\n    prev=json.loads((old/name/\\\"item.json\\\").read_text())\n    print(name,len(it[\\\"passages\\\"]),[k for k in it.keys()|prev.keys() if it.get(k)\"'!=prev.get(k)],hashlib.sha256(it[\"system\"].encode()).hexdigest()==b.REQUEST_SHA256)\nfor f in (\"judging-v13s.txt\",\"judging-v13s-d749.txt\"):\n    print(f,len(Path(f).read_text()),len(Path(f).read_bytes()))'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"S 50 ['system'] True\nE 15 ['system'] True\nP-v8 296 ['system'] True\njudging-v13s.txt 4867 4867\njudging-v13s-d749.txt 4794 4794\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba codex-r64.md | tail -18; rg -n 'split|invented|vote' claude-r64.md; cat ../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X07.codex.json | head -18; nl -ba blind5.py | sed -n '128,150p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba codex-r64.md | tail -18; rg -n 'split|invented|vote' claude-r64.md; cat ../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X07.codex.json | head -18; nl -ba blind5.py | sed -n '128,150p'\"","aggregated_output":"    19\t   | “The passages give its size, a bound that justifies the rung” | The bound is **consistent**. “Give its size” **keeps narrower** than D745 if read to require a stated size. D745 accepts passage evidence *supporting* the writer’s estimate. [D745:11517](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11517) |\n    20\t   | “Or a related figure,” with an explanation covering companies, period, quantity and the writer’s own assumptions | The explanation is **consistent**. The unqualified “or” **keeps wider** than D747. Alex permits this route when the passages do not give the quantity asked. The readers receive that qualification explicitly. [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582) · [Reader definition:12](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:12) |\n    21\t   | Answer unresolved and name what is unknown when neither side is favoured. | **Consistent** with D745, D749 and `answers/why`. “Not established … never a reason to stay” concerns confidence. “Missing confirmation alone” bars using silence to select an extreme. Neither requires picking a band when the strongest evidence leaves the choice open. [D745:11515](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11515) · [System:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:5) · [Schema:324](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:324) |\n    22\t   | Guidance alone keeps the reference rung. The two discount sentences concern unshipped products. | **Consistent when read with bullet 2’s express condition**, but bullet 3 independently says the target “is an input to be discounted.” That imperative invites the old sequence on E. Guidance identifies the reference. Execution risk matters, but its existence alone cannot justify crossing a boundary. Make bullet 3 refer to the same condition. [System:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [System:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:5) · [D745:11518](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11518) |\n    23\t\n    24\t   A shorter replacement for the two changed sentences is: “Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If the strongest evidence leaves the band unsettled, answer unresolved and explain the gap.” [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582) · [D749:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645)\n    25\t\n    26\t4. **Minor — The three cases still discriminate, with one limit.** These are predictions from saved replies, not fresh writer results.\n    27\t\n    28\t   - **E.** Twice $20B is the $40B boundary. Management said *more than* double, with the excess unspecified. The two saved Codex replies produced $34B and $36B from unsupported 15% and 10% adjustments. The later Claude reply’s lower comparison gives $27B at its own 90% share, outside its chosen $30–40B band. On those arguments I would expect a specific unresolved answer. A forecast remains possible with an explained comparison. The earlier $37B Claude forecast is the live boundary case: its 85% growth explanation still divided readers under D747. [E evidence:10](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:10) · [Earlier Codex:178](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json:178) · [Later Codex:130](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:130) · [Later Claude:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Earlier Claude:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [Re-read:96](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:96)\n    29\t   - **S.** At $745B total spending, the four firms can spend up to $45B outside data centers and still reach $700B. U08 used the same firms’ AI spending and Meta’s spending mix to explain a $25B deduction. Both readers accepted that forecast. They also accepted U04’s specific explanation of why the category difference left the cutoff unsettled. Either answer can pass. U02’s impossible floor cannot. [U08 deduction:226](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Recorded reasons:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-reasons.json:5) · [Floor finding:99](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:99)\n    30\t   - **P-v8.** I expect a forecast. Reported first-half revenue of about $164.3B, next-quarter guidance and the observed 92.5% segment share support the two saved forecasts near $385.6B, inside $350–400B. Both had unanimous support. Requiring two fresh forecasts catches withholding on this supported case. The test nevertheless allows **all four fresh E and S replies** to be unresolved if their reasons earn agreement. A pass therefore cannot establish how often the writer withholds on borderline cases. [Codex P-v8:314](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/codex.json:314) · [Claude P-v8:314](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/claude.json:314) · [Prior votes:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:15) · [Pass rules:474](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:474)\n    31\t\n    32\t5. **Minor — Packet provenance is correct. Tighten the two verification checks.** I rebuilt S, E and P-v8 in memory. All three had the exact current system text, and their user text and schemas matched the saved D745 cases. Both initial reading and default adjudication load each control’s **own** system text, user text, schema descriptions and qualifier list. U05 is the original invented-cut answer and must earn a settled **no**. U02 is the original floor answer and its support vote is correctly excluded. The build guard itself checks only a substring, so it would also accept an incomplete repair retaining those words. Compare the complete registered text. [Control key:7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-key.json:7) · [Control key:22](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-key.json:22) · [Build check:32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:32) · [Packet builder:49](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:49) · [Format material:63](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:63) · [Adjudication:64](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:64)\n    33\t\n    34\t   The reasons record correctly sends matching first votes with incompatible explanations to adjudication and waits for a compatibility record before accepting matching final votes. First votes are validated as `yes` or `no`; adjudicator votes are not. Apply the same validation at both stages, and retain Claude’s short explanation of compatibility as in the saved record. [Reasons handling:108](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:108) · [Saved reasons:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-reasons.json:4)\n    35\t\n    36\t**Verdict — send with listed changes.** The test can change the next decision. I checked in memory that a specific E unresolved answer, the saved justified S unresolved answer and a supported P-v8 forecast all pass the schema and publication checks. Once the tally distinguishes failure from disagreement, a clean pass supports **asking Alex** about the paid re-send. A failure or persistent split returns to council. [Registration:474](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:474) · [Alex’s authorization:11652](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11652)rg: claude-r64.md: IO error for operation on claude-r64.md: No such file or directory (os error 2)\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"why\": \"On F11’s $720 billion total, reaching $700 billion requires all four companies together to spend no more than $20 billion outside data centers. F14 assumes a 98% data-center share, but e5014 says only that “a large portion” goes to AI infrastructure. e1947 covers the right four companies and year, but the answer does not explain how much of their AI infrastructure spending counts as data-center capex. Meta’s $130 billion covers one company, Amazon’s memory costs explain its increase, and the $750 billion data-center estimates do not isolate these four companies. At the answer’s own 96% lower estimate, spending is $691.2 billion. The guidance raises support the total but do not explain why the share needed for rung 4 is more likely.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n      \"type\": \"part above or added into its whole\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F12\", \"F11\", \"F15\"],\n      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n      \"why\": \"The answer adopts e1947’s “more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year” as a “supported floor” for data-center capex. That puts the minimum for this part above F11’s $720 billion total for the same four companies and year. It also puts F15’s $705.6 billion forecast below its own floor. The writer’s rule says “a part cannot exceed its whole.”\"\n    }\n  ]\n}   128\t        if fresh and item['case'] == 'P-v8':\n   129\t            for a in answer['answers']:\n   130\t                if a['shape'] != 'forecast':\n   131\t                    fails.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]}: {a[\"view_id\"]} is {a[\"shape\"]}, not a forecast')\n   132\t        first = [b4._reading(out / 'reader-packets' / f'{pid}.{w}.json') for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n   133\t        adjudicated = [out / 'adjudication' / f'{pid}.{w}.json' for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n   134\t        adj = [b4._reading(p) for p in adjudicated] if all(p.exists() for p in adjudicated) else None\n   135\t        record = reasons.get(pid, {})\n   136\t        marks = [_keys(r, floor) for r in first]\n   137\t        disputed = (marks[0] | marks[1]) - (marks[0] & marks[1])\n   138\t        defect_state = {d: 'settled' for d in marks[0] & marks[1]}\n   139\t        if disputed and adj is None:\n   140\t            need.append(f'{pid} (a defect marked by one reader)')\n   141\t        elif disputed:\n   142\t            decisions = [_decisions(x, floor) for x in adj]\n   143\t            for d in disputed:\n   144\t                pair = [decisions[0].get(d), decisions[1].get(d)]\n   145\t                defect_state[d] = 'settled' if pair == ['uphold', 'uphold'] else 'rejected' if pair == ['reject', 'reject'] else 'split'\n   146\t        for d, state in defect_state.items():\n   147\t            row = [pid, item['label'], d[0], list(d[1]), state]\n   148\t            (settled_defects if state == 'settled' else split_defects if state == 'split' else []).append(row)\n   149\t            if fresh and state == 'settled':\n   150\t                fails.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]}: settled defect {d[0]} {list(d[1])}')\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Reader [12] on member|# The two readings' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X08.md | cut -c1-1200\nsed -n '1,16p' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r64.md\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/TALLY.json | head -34\nnl -ba docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | sed -n '448,464p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Reader [12] on member|# The two readings' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X08.md | cut -c1-1200\nsed -n '1,16p' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r64.md\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/TALLY.json | head -34\nnl -ba docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | sed -n '448,464p'\"","aggregated_output":"40:# The two readings\n41:- Reader 1 on member v6: supported = yes. Ground: At F16’s $740 billion total, the four companies can spend up to $40 billion outside data centers and still reach rung 4. The strongest comparison is e1947, which puts the same four companies’ indicated 2026 AI infrastructure spending above $730 billion. The writer treats that spending as a guide to data-center capex. Meta’s $130 billion AI plan against $135 billion in midpoint total guidance (e3686, e1549) and the memory costs behind Amazon’s increase (e1649) support that interpretation. The 96% share is the writer’s estimate. It leaves $29.6 billion outside data centers and puts the forecast at $710.4 billion.\n43:- Reader 2 on member v6: supported = yes. Ground: The band is decided by the data-center share of total capex (F17, 96%) applied to a $740bn total (F16). The total sits inside the guided/aggregated $720-745bn range (e5014, e2079) and just below later trackers (e1856 near $745bn, e2458 $760bn), well inside rung 4 before any deduction. The share is an explained related figure: e1947 has the same four companies indicating more than $730bn of AI-infrastructure spending in 2026, and the answer explains that AI infrastructure is contained in data-center capex, so data-center capex is at least that (above the $700bn line). It adds Meta's $130bn of AI capex (e3686) against the $135bn midpoint of its $125-145bn guidance (e1549), which is about 96% before any non-AI data centers. It notes that Amazon's raise came from server memory costs (e1649, e4567), and it cites the about $750bn data-center spending estimates (e1416, e4082). The answer states that rung 3 needs a non-data-center remainder above about 5% (about $40bn) and admits the margin is thin. Under its stated assumptions, rung 4 is the more likely side.\n**Send with the changes below.** The repair asks the right question on E: what makes sales below $40B more likely? The main blockers are in the tally. I reproduced a clean `pass` with a fresh defect still disputed, and an `inconclusive` result when both readers caught the registered $730B floor. No writer was sent and no file was edited. [D749 decision:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645) · [Tally:95](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:95)\n\n1. **Serious — The floor control can miss agreement.** U02 calls more than $730B a floor for data-center spending while forecasting just $720B of *total* spending. The registered reading is the defect type “part above or added into its whole” naming **F12**. Its support vote is immaterial. The tally first compares the complete lists of figure IDs. With one reader naming `F12` and the other naming `F11, F12, F15`, it treats their common finding as two disputes. In an in-memory replay where both adjudicators upheld the floor using those respective lists, it returned `inconclusive`. Match this control by **type and presence of F12 before comparing votes**, at both reading stages. Keep the full lists in the report. [U02 answer:390](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [Registration:473](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:473) · [Figure-list comparison:159](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:159) · [Floor check:143](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:143)\n\n2. **Serious — Three outcomes need fixing before the send.** I exercised each in memory, with the other votes set to pass.\n\n   | Case | Current result | Required change |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | A fresh answer earns support, but adjudicators remain divided on a reported arithmetic defect | `pass`, with the defect still listed as split | D748 sends a persistent split back to council. The registration explicitly covers support and floor splits but leaves this case open. Register a persistent fresh-defect split as inconclusive and apply it in the tally. [D748:11637](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11637) · [Registration:480](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:480) · [Tally:150](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:150) |\n   | A P-v8 writer returns `unresolved` and readers split on its reason | `inconclusive` | Fail as soon as the answer shape is known. Alex requires **two forecasts** on P-v8. Agreement about a reason cannot supply the missing forecast. [D748:11634](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11634) · [Tally:133](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:133) |\n   | A fresh first reply is invalid JSON | The checker records the refusal, but packet construction parses the reply again and raises `JSONDecodeError`. The tally also reparses it before reaching the refusal check. | Record `fail` from the checked refusal before requiring a readable answer or reader votes. The same rule covers a schema refusal that leaves no usable `answers` field. [First-reply check:69](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:69) · [Packet construction:62](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:62) · [Tally:90](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:90) |\n\n3. **Minor — Two clauses in line 9 drift from the decisions.** The change is general. It names no company, product, amount or test case. The growth has a written reason: the earlier writer lacked D747’s comparison route and D749’s order of decision. I verified 4,540 → 4,816 system-text characters, or 910 → 972 tokens using `o200k_base`. The registration records only the character counts. [Old line 9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s-d745.txt:9) · [New line 9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:9) · [Growth rationale:448](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:448) · [Prompt rule:223](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:223)\n\n   | Clause | Audit against the decisions and the rest of the request |\n   |---|---|\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"verdict\": \"inconclusive\",\n     3\t \"fails\": [],\n     4\t \"splits\": [\n     5\t  \"X05 fresh E/claude: v41 settled=split [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer]\",\n     6\t  \"X08 fresh S/claude: v6 settled=split [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer]\"\n     7\t ],\n     8\t \"lines\": [\n     9\t  \"X01 fresh P-v8/claude      v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\",\n    10\t  \"X02 fresh S/codex          v6 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] ok\",\n    11\t  \"X03 K-invented-cut         v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [control expects not supported] ok\",\n    12\t  \"X04 fresh P-v8/codex       v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\",\n    13\t  \"X05 fresh E/claude         v41 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n    14\t  \"X06 fresh E/codex          v41 unresolved settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] ok\",\n    15\t  \"X07 K-impossible-floor     v6 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [support not scored; the floor defect is] ok\",\n    16\t  \"X08 fresh S/claude         v6 forecast   settled=split  votes=['yes', 'yes', 'yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\"\n    17\t ],\n    18\t \"settled_defects\": [\n    19\t  [\n    20\t   \"X07\",\n    21\t   \"K-impossible-floor\",\n    22\t   \"part above or added into its whole\",\n    23\t   [\n    24\t    \"F12\"\n    25\t   ],\n    26\t   \"settled\"\n    27\t  ]\n    28\t ],\n    29\t \"split_defects\": [],\n    30\t \"floor_control\": \"settled\"\n    31\t}   448\t**The one next task: the second $0 writer test (D750), after council round 66 reviews it.** Alex is asleep and handed decisions to\n   449\tClaude and the council (D439: reversible, $0, nothing committed).\n   450\t\n   451\t- **The D749 test was INCONCLUSIVE, with no failed line** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND5-RESULT.md`).\n   452\t  - E's Codex writer now withholds with a specific gap.\n   453\t  - S Codex and both P-v8 replies are supported.\n   454\t  - Both controls were caught.\n   455\t  - Two splits: E Claude takes a not-yet-shipping target in full without evidence of volume in hand; S Claude's readers disagree on a\n   456\t    floor above its own outcome.\n   457\t- **The change** (council 65), at bullet 2: a target \"remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what\n   458\t  evidence puts the required volume in hand for the period\".\n   459\t- **The test.** The same design on S, E and P-v8 (`blind6.py`; `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D750]).\n   460\t- **Held.** The paid re-send, until a clean pass. Then Alex authorizes.\n   461\t\n   462\tThe record of the D749 step:\n   463\t\n   464\t**Earlier next task (done, D750): the $0 writer test of the E writer repair (D749), after council round 64 reviews the request\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
